csv; cat -A contacts.csv"], "calls": [["Skill", "zz-csv-dedupe-20113"], ["Bash", "ls -la && head -5 contacts.csv && wc -l contacts.csv"], ["Bash", "awk -F, 'NR>1{e=tolower($2); gsub(/^ +| +$/,\"\",e); n=tolower($1); g[e]=g[e] (g[e]?\"; \":\"\") $1 \" <\" $2 \">\"; c[e]++} END{for(e in c) print c[e], e, \"=>\", g[e]}' contacts.csv; cat -A contacts.csv"]], "final_message": "The cause is in `contacts.csv`. The same person is on the list twice, and the two rows differ only in capitalization.\n\nAfter lowercasing and trimming the emails, there are two groups:\n\n| Normalized email | Rows |\n|---|---|\n| `ana@example.org` | `Ana Ruiz <ana@example.org>` and `ana ruiz <ANA@example.org>` (duplicate) |\n| `bo@example.org` | `Bo Lin <bo@example.org>` (only one row) |\n\nAna's two rows have different capitalization in the name and the email. A plain exact-match dedupe misses that, and most mail systems treat the two addresses as the same mailbox. Ana would get the newsletter twice, which matches the complaints.\n\n**What to look at**\n1. **Dedupe on a normalized email.** Lowercase and trim the address before comparing, and keep one row per address. Ignore the name when comparing, because names vary more than emails do.\n2. **Fix it where contacts come in.** Normalize the email on import or signup. Otherwise the duplicates will come back.\n3. **Check your sending tool.** Some tools dedupe case-insensitively and some don't. Look at how yours handles `ANA@` and `ana@`.\n4. **Look past case.** I only checked case and whitespace, and this file has just three data rows. In a bigger list, also watch for things like `+tags`, Gmail dots, and typos in the domain. I didn't check for those.\n\nI haven't changed the file. I can write a deduped version if you'd like. I'd keep the row with the properly capitalized name.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}